Sure, here is your Python code translated to Go. I'll use the "container/heap" standard library for the heap operations.

```go
package main

import (
	"container/heap"
)

// create a new type to implement heap.Interface
type Path []([3]int)

func (p Path) Len() int {
	return len(p)
}

func (p Path) Less(i, j int) bool {
	return p[i][0] < p[j][0] // compare the first elements
}

func (p Path) Swap(i, j int) {
	p[i], p[j] = p[j], p[i]
}

func (p *Path) Push(x interface{}) {
	*p = append(*p, x.([3]int))
}

func (p *Path) Pop() interface{} {
	old := *p
	n := len(old)
	x := old[n-1]
	*p = old[0 : n-1]
	return x
}

func minPath(grid [][]int, k int) []int {
	N := len(grid)

	minHeap := &Path{[3]int{grid[0][0], 0, 0}}
	heap.Init(minHeap) // initializing our heap

	visited := make(map[[2]int]bool)
	visited[[2]int{0, 0}] = true

	directions := [4][2]int{{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}}

	res := []int{}

	for k > 0 {
		val := heap.Pop(minHeap).([3]int) // pop the smallest value from heap
		res = append(res, val[0])
		for _, d := range directions {
			nx, ny := val[1]+d[0], val[2]+d[1]
			if nx >= 0 && nx < N && ny >= 0 && ny < N {
				if !visited[[2]int{nx, ny}] {
					visited[[2]int{nx, ny}] = true
					heap.Push(minHeap, [3]int{grid[nx][ny], nx, ny})
				}
			}
		}
		k--
	}
	return res
}
```

- Here is a brief explanation of what we did here. We map the heapq algorithm in Python to heap algorithm in Go by implementing the heap.Interface. This Interface requires to define 5 methods: Len, Less, Swap, Push, and Pop.
- We pass an array with three integer elements into the heap, and we define the Less method as such to make the smallest item comes at the top of the heap.
- In Go, we dont have sets, but we can mimic the sets using maps. Here, we create a map with array of two integer keys, and the value is boolean indicating whether the cell is visited or not.
- We have to initialize the heap before using. We do this using the heap.Init function.
- heap.Pop will remove and return the minimum element (root) from the heap. heap.Push will insert the new value into the heap maintaining the heap property.
- The slice is dynamic in Go and we keep appending to the slice by using the append function.
- There is no decrementing operation in Go like Python, so we use k-- to decrement.

