Here is the code that solves the problem:

```c++
#include<stdio.h>
#include<vector>

using namespace std;

pair<int, int> arrange_checker(vector<int> arr) {
    // iterate over the vector to check element by element
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.size() - 1; i++) {
        // if current element is greater than next one
        if (arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {
            // return a pair with index and difference
            return {i, arr[i] - arr[i + 1]};
        }
    }

    // if no such element was found, return -1
    return {-1, -1};
}
```

This function works by iterating over the vector and checking whether each element is greater than the one following it. If it finds such an element, it returns its index and the difference between the two numbers. The time complexity of this function is O(N) because in the worst-case scenario it has to check each element of the vector once. If no element that meets the criteria is found, the function returns a pair of -1, -1.

