Dynamic programming is a method for solving complex problems by breaking them down into simpler subproblems and using the fact that the optimal solution to the overall problem depends upon the optimal solution to its subproblems.

Here's a simple solution to the Fibonacci series using Dynamic Programming in C++:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

int Fib(int n)
{
    // Create an array of size n+1 to store the Fibonacci numbers.
    vector<int> f(n + 1, 0);

    // Base cases:
    f[0] = 0;
    if(n > 0) 
        f[1] = 1;

    // Calculate the Fibonacci sequence from 2 to n.
    for(int i = 2; i <= n; i++)
    {
        f[i] = f[i - 1] + f[i - 2];
    }

    return f[n]; // return the nth Fibonacci number
}

int main()
{
    int n;
    cout << "Enter a number: ";
    cin >> n;
    cout << "Fibonacci number is: " << Fib(n);
    return 0;
}
```

This program starts with an array of size n+1 all initialized to 0 (using the vector constructor that takes size and initial value as arguments). It then sets the base cases (f[0] and f[1]) and fill up the rest of the array in bottom-up fashion by adding the previous two numbers. Finally, it returns the nth Fibonacci number.

This solution is efficient since it only needs to calculate each Fibonacci number once, storing it in the array for later reference instead of recalculating it every time. The time complexity is O(n) because each number from 2 to n is visited once. The space complexity is also O(n) because of the array of size n+1.

