To create a Trie data structure optimized for common prefixes with efficient insertion, search, and deletion, we design the Trie using nodes with children represented as an array for quick access. Each node tracks whether it marks the end of a word. Deletion uses a recursive helper to prune unnecessary nodes.

```java
import java.util.TreeMap;

class TrieNode {
    private static final int ALPHABET_SIZE = 26;
    private TrieNode[] children;
    private boolean isEndOfWord;

    public TrieNode() {
        children = new TrieNode[ALPHABET_SIZE];
        isEndOfWord = false;
    }
}

public class Trie {
    private TrieNode root;

    public Trie() {
        root = new TrieNode();
    }

    // Insert a word into the trie
    public void insert(String word) {
        TrieNode current = root;
        for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {
            int index = c - 'a';
            if (current.children[index] == null) {
                current.children[index] = new TrieNode();
            }
            current = current.children[index];
        }
        current.isEndOfWord = true;
    }

    // Search for a word in the trie
    public boolean search(String word) {
        TrieNode current = root;
        for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {
            int index = c - 'a';
            if (current.children[index] == null) return false;
            current = current.children[index];
        }
        return current.isEndOfWord;
    }

    // Delete a word from the trie
    public void delete(String word) {
        root = deleteHelper(root, word, 0);
    }

    private TrieNode deleteHelper(TrieNode node, String word, int index) {
        if (node == null) return null;

        if (index == word.length()) {
            if (node.isEndOfWord)
                node.isEndOfWord = false;

            // Check if there are any children remaining
            if (node.children == null || 
                !hasAnyChildren(node.children)) { 
                return null;
            }
            return node;
        }

        int cIndex = word.charAt(index) - 'a';
        TrieNode child = node.children[cIndex];
        TrieNode removedChild = deleteHelper(child, word, index + 1);
        node.children[cIndex] = removedChild; // set null if removed

        // Check if this node should be pruned now
        if (!node.isEndOfWord && 
            (node.children == null || 
             !hasAnyChildren(node.children))) {
            return null;
        }
        return node;
    }

    private boolean hasAnyChildren(TrieNode[] children) {
        for (TrieNode child : children) {
            if (child != null) return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    // Example usage with specified keywords
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Trie trie = new Trie();
        trie.insert("basis");
        trie.insert("kid");
        trie.insert("sick");
        trie.insert("signal");

        System.out.println("Search 'basis': " + trie.search("basis")); // true
        System.out.println("Search 'sick': " + trie.search("sick")); // true
        System.out.println("Search 'kid': " + trie.search("kid")); // true

        trie.delete("kid");
        System.out.println("Search 'kid' after deletion: " + trie.search("kid")); // false
    }
}
```

### Explanation:
1. **TrieNode Structure**: Each node contains an array of 26 characters (for `a-z`), allowing O(1) lookups instead of hash tables, improving efficiency.
2. **Insertion**: Iterates over each character of the word, creates nodes for new paths, and marks the end node.
3. **Search**: Checks each character path for existence and the end marker.
4. **Deletion**: Uses a helper method to recursively navigate the trie, unmarks the end node, and removes nodes when they no longer have children or serve as an end point.
5. **Efficiency**: Operations are O(L) where L is the word length due to array access patterns and pruning.

The included keywords ['basis', 'kid', 'sick', 'signal'] are inserted, searched, and demonstrated with deletion to illustrate functionality.

