Wrapping an SVG path around a circle is not a straightforward task and generally will involve some tricky mathematics or defining a new SVG with the desired curvature. One common way this is done is by manually editing the SVG paths using a design tool like Adobe Illustrator or Inkscape. 
To do this most reliably, you would need to:

1. Create a circle with a circumference equal to the length of the path (also known as the path length).
2. Cut the path at the points where it needs to wrap around the circle.
3. Position each section of the path along the circumference of the circle using transformations - translate and rotate primarily.

However, there's a quicker workaround to wrap SVG elements around a circle in HTML using CSS transformations which are relatively easy to apply. This may not produce perfect results, but it can give the essence of a wrapped SVG. The core idea is to create equal-sized child elements for every segment of the desired circle and then use `rotate` and `translate` to position these elements around the circle.

Here's a simple example:

```html
<div class="circle">
    <div class="seg1"></div>
    <div class="seg2"></div>
    <div class="seg3"></div>
    <!-- More segments as required -->
</div>
```
```css
.circle {
